<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><sup id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">a</span></sup><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Best</span><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> (cell shaded </span><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">light blue</span><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">).  </span><sup id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">b</span></sup><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Second-best</span><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> (cell shaded </span><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">light green</span><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">). </span>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations of Current Evaluation Practices.</span>
Several recent studies have noted that the high scores reported on LIBERO often fail to translate into reliable performance, even on tasks with only minor variations. This has raised serious concerns about the validity of its evaluation protocol<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li (2025)</a></cite>. In response, some researchers have explored stronger algorithms or proposed stress tests <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guruprasad et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Valle et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kube et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025a)</a></cite>to encourage more robust VLA systems. However, the field still lacks a systematic and standardized framework for robust evaluation under LIBERO. The majority of works continue to rely on the original, flawed setup, reporting numbers that may not reflect genuine capability. To address this gap, we propose LIBERO-PRO, a plug-and-play extension of LIBERO designed to foster fair comparison across models and to advance research on building VLA systems that can withstand reasonable and practically necessary perturbations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Problem Definition</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section formalizes the Vision-Language-Action (VLA) task framework and examines limitations in the LIBERO benchmark’s evaluation protocol that may obscure true task competence.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Formal Task Definition</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define a VLA task to establish a rigorous basis for evaluating robotic systems.</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(\ell,\mathcal{O},\mathcal{E},p_{0},G)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(\ell,\mathcal{O},\mathcal{E},p_{0},G),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is a natural language instruction specifying the task objective, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set of objects with attributes (e.g., shape, color, size, initial pose), <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the static environmental context (e.g., scene geometry, furniture layout), <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a probability distribution over initial states <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (encoding init position and robot proprioception), and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is a binary goal predicate, returning <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> if state <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The performance of a policy <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(a\mid s,\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(a\mid s,\ell)</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterized by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, is measured by the expected success probability over a finite horizon <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{s_{0}\sim p_{0},\pi_{\theta}}\left[G(s_{T})=1\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{s_{0}\sim p_{0},\pi_{\theta}}\left[G(s_{T})=1\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the terminal state after <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps. This formulation captures the integration of perception, language understanding, and action planning in VLA systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Current LIBERO Evaluation Protocol</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LIBERO benchmark, while valuable for VLA research, employs an evaluation protocol that may overestimate model competence due to its homogeneous train/test split. Each test task <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\prime}\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>test</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\prime}\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> mirrors a training task <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>train</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\prime}=\ell,\quad\mathcal{O}^{\prime}=\mathcal{O},\quad\mathcal{E}^{\prime}=\mathcal{E},\quad G^{\prime}=G," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\prime}=\ell,\quad\mathcal{O}^{\prime}=\mathcal{O},\quad\mathcal{E}^{\prime}=\mathcal{E},\quad G^{\prime}=G,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with the only difference being a minor perturbation in the initial state distribution:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{TV}}(p_{0},p_{0}^{\prime})\leq\epsilon,\quad\epsilon\ll 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{TV}}(p_{0},p_{0}^{\prime})\leq\epsilon,\quad\epsilon\ll 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Due to the small <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, test tasks closely resemble training tasks, allowing policies to achieve high performance through memorization rather than robust generalization. This homogeneity may inflate performance metrics, masking limitations in compositional generalization and adaptability to diverse real-world scenarios, a critical concern for embodied AI.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03827v1/main_figure.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/285;" width="471" height="285" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">LIBERO-PRO benchmark task overview. We extend the four original task categories in LIBERO by introducing object attribute perturbations, initial position perturbations, instruction perturbations, and environmental perturbations.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Proposed Perturbation-Based Evaluation Framework</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a more reasonable evaluation of VLA models, we propose a framework that introduces controlled perturbations across four orthogonal dimensions, manipulated objects, initial states, task instructions, and environments. For a task <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(\ell,\mathcal{O},\mathcal{E},p_{0},G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(\ell,\mathcal{O},\mathcal{E},p_{0},G)</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate perturbed tasks <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{(k)}=\phi_{k}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{(k)}=\phi_{k}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> using operators <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{\mathrm{O},\mathrm{S},\mathrm{L},\mathrm{E}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{\mathrm{O},\mathrm{S},\mathrm{L},\mathrm{E}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Object Attribute Perturbation (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\mathrm{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\mathrm{O}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>: Modifies non-essential object attributes, such as color, texture, or size, while preserving semantic equivalence, e.g., changing a red cup to a blue cup. This tests the model’s robustness to superficial visual changes critical for real-world adaptability. The perturbed task is:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{(\mathrm{O})}=\left(\ell,\mathcal{O}^{(\mathrm{O})},\mathcal{E},p_{0},G\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{(\mathrm{O})}=\left(\ell,\mathcal{O}^{(\mathrm{O})},\mathcal{E},p_{0},G\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initial Position Perturbation (<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\mathrm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\mathrm{I}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>: Alters initial object positions to modify absolute and relative arrangements, ensuring physical plausibility, e.g., moving a cup relative to a plate. This probes the model’s spatial reasoning under varied layouts. The perturbed task is:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{(\mathrm{I})}=\left(\ell,\mathcal{O},\mathcal{E},p_{0}^{(\mathrm{I})},G\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{(\mathrm{I})}=\left(\ell,\mathcal{O},\mathcal{E},p_{0}^{(\mathrm{I})},G\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Instruction Perturbation (<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\mathrm{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\mathrm{L}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>: Perturbations are applied at both the semantic and task levels. Semantic perturbations adjust the linguistic expression while preserving the original task intent, such as paraphrasing “pick up the bottle” to “grab the bottle” or substituting with a jar. This evaluates the agent’s understanding of language and semantics. The perturbed task is:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{(\mathrm{L})}=\left(\ell^{(\mathrm{L})},\mathcal{O},\mathcal{E},p_{0},G\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{(\mathrm{L})}=\left(\ell^{(\mathrm{L})},\mathcal{O},\mathcal{E},p_{0},G\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Task perturbations reasonably modify the task itself by replacing the target object or altering the action, e.g., changing “pick up the bottle” to “pick up the bowl.” We ensure that all actions and objects appear in the training set to support fair and meaningful generalization. The perturbed task is:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{(\mathrm{L})}=\left(\ell^{(\mathrm{L})},\mathcal{O}^{(\mathrm{L})},\mathcal{E},p_{0},G^{(\mathrm{L})}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{(\mathrm{L})}=\left(\ell^{(\mathrm{L})},\mathcal{O}^{(\mathrm{L})},\mathcal{E},p_{0},G^{(\mathrm{L})}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Environment Perturbation (<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\mathrm{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\mathrm{E}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>: Modifies the background’s appearance, e.g., changing a workspace, without affecting task feasibility, to assess robustness to environmental variations. The perturbed task is:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{(\mathrm{E})}=\left(\ell,\mathcal{O},\mathcal{E}^{(\mathrm{E})},p,G\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{(\mathrm{E})}=\left(\ell,\mathcal{O},\mathcal{E}^{(\mathrm{E})},p,G\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Perturbation Constraints</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure each perturbed task <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> remains semantically valid, we impose two constraints.
First, perturbations are confined within a dimension-specific neighborhood:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(k)}\in\mathcal{N}_{k}(x),\quad\mathrm{dist}_{k}(x,x^{(k)})\leq\delta_{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(k)}\in\mathcal{N}_{k}(x),\quad\mathrm{dist}_{k}(x,x^{(k)})\leq\delta_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is the original component, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> is its perturbed version, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> controls the magnitude of perturbation for dimension <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{\mathrm{O},\mathrm{S},\mathrm{L},\mathrm{E}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{\mathrm{O},\mathrm{S},\mathrm{L},\mathrm{E}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Second, we enforce non-trivial task-level separation:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{TV}}(\tau,\tau^{(k)})&gt;\epsilon,\quad\forall k\in\{\mathrm{O},\mathrm{S},\mathrm{L},\mathrm{E}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{TV}}(\tau,\tau^{(k)})&gt;\epsilon,\quad\forall k\in\{\mathrm{O},\mathrm{S},\mathrm{L},\mathrm{E}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{TV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{TV}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the total variation distance, ensuring that perturbations lead to substantive deviations.
Multiple perturbed tasks are generated per category by applying <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> varied to characterize the sensitivity of policies under different perturbation magnitudes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>LIBERO-PRO Evaluation Benchmark</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on these principles, we introduce LIBERO-PRO, a plug-and-play evaluation benchmark that enables systematic assessment under multiple dimensions of variation—object, position, instruction, task, and environment—as well as their arbitrary combinations, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Current LIBERO Evaluation Protocol ‣ 3 Problem Definition ‣ LIBERO-PRO: Towards Robust and Fair Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Models Beyond Memorization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Specifically, for object variation, we create new assets for the four LIBERO tasks by modifying the appearance, size, and color of the original objects. For position variation, we define alternative spatial regions for all manipulable objects while strictly adhering to physical constraints and task definitions. For instruction variation, we provide three distinct paraphrased versions of each task description. For task variation, we redesign tasks in a principled manner to ensure feasibility, and supply new sets of relevant objects and target states for each modified task. For environment variation, we leverage the five environment categories built into the LIBERO benchmark and randomly substitute them across tasks. To avoid meaningless combinations, we restrict task variation from being combined with other types, while allowing all other dimensions of variation to be freely and randomly combined for more comprehensive evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Settings</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments on LIBERO-PRO, which applies controlled perturbations from four perspectives: object properties, initial positions, task instructions (covering both semantic and structural variations), and environments. We evaluate three representative models—OpenVLA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2024)</a></cite>, pi0<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Black et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and pi0.5<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Intelligence et al. (2025)</a></cite>. For fairness, OpenVLA is assessed using four official checkpoints trained on different LIBERO suites, whereas pi0 and pi0.5 are evaluated with a single official checkpoint. Consistent with the original LIBERO protocol, we set the number of evaluation episodes to 50 per task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted a comprehensive evaluation of state-of-the-art vision-language-action (VLA) models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Black et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2024)</a></cite> using the LIBERO-PRO evaluation suite, which introduces perturbations across four dimensions: objects, goals, spatial configurations, and task sets (see Tables <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2 Main Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ LIBERO-PRO: Towards Robust and Fair Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Models Beyond Memorization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Main Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ LIBERO-PRO: Towards Robust and Fair Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Models Beyond Memorization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.2 Main Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ LIBERO-PRO: Towards Robust and Fair Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Models Beyond Memorization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.2 Main Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ LIBERO-PRO: Towards Robust and Fair Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Models Beyond Memorization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Our findings are as follows: (1) <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Failure under simple perturbations.</span> Despite achieving success rates above 90% on the standard LIBERO benchmark, models nearly collapse under changes to object positions or minor task modifications, even when constructed from training components. (2) <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Illusory robustness.</span> Apparent stability against object and instruction perturbations is largely due to overfitting rather than genuine task understanding. (3) <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model-specific sensitivity.</span> Environmental perturbations affect models differently, depending on the degree of environmental divergence and reliance on memorized contexts. (4) <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subtle capability differences.</span> While most models fail under perturbations, Pi0.5 achieves a 0.38 success rate in the libero-goal task under position changes, compared to 0 for OpenVLA and Pi0, revealing differences masked by standard benchmark scores. Overall, these results show that current VLA models, despite excelling on the standard LIBERO, lack true robustness and generalization—highlighting the need for rigorous evaluation frameworks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.T2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Task-wise LIBERO and LIBERO-PRO performance of three VLA models on the <span id="S5.T2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">libero-goal</span> benchmark. Accuracy on test (Ori: origina, Obj: object, Spa: spatial, Sem: semantic, Task: task-level, Env: environment).
<span id="S5.T2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Action notation:</span> <math id="S5.T2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Open(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Open(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> = open target <math id="S5.T2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> of container <math id="S5.T2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="S5.T2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Put(obj,loc)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Put(obj,loc)</annotation></semantics></math> = place object <math id="S5.T2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="obj" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">obj</annotation></semantics></math> onto/into location <math id="S5.T2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="loc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">loc</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="S5.T2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Push(obj,loc)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Push(obj,loc)</annotation></semantics></math> = push object <math id="S5.T2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="obj" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">obj</annotation></semantics></math> toward location <math id="S5.T2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="loc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">loc</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="S5.T2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TurnOn(obj)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TurnOn(obj)</annotation></semantics></math> = activate object <math id="S5.T2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="obj" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">obj</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
