<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Does the Model Generalize to New Objects?</h3>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:55%;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03827v1/object_ood_1.png" id="S5.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:262/71;" width="262" height="71" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">"salad dressing" in the training set.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03827v1/object_ood_2.png" id="S5.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:262/71;" width="262" height="71" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">"salad dressing" not in the training set.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03827v1/object_ood_3.png" id="S5.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:262/72;" width="262" height="72" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S5.F4.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Replace "salad dressing" with "alphabet soup".</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pick up salad dressing and place it in basket.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate whether the VLA model exhibits true object-level generalization, we conducted tests by altering the manipulated object in the task “pick up the salad dressing and place it in the basket”. As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3 Does the Model Generalize to New Objects? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ LIBERO-PRO: Towards Robust and Fair Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Models Beyond Memorization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, replacing the original salad dressing with an unseen but visually similar variant from outside the training set (second row) did not affect the model’s performance, suggesting a limited degree of generalization to perturbed object instances. However, when substituting salad dressing with an unrelated object (e.g., alphabet soup, third row), the model still executed the same action trajectory, attempting to pick up the new object despite its mismatch with the instruction. This behavior suggests that the model’s apparent generalization is largely driven by rote recall of action trajectories from training rather than genuine grounding of object identity. Moreover, its failure on semantically irrelevant objects highlights the inability to correctly align linguistic references with object semantics. Taken together, these observations indicate that the model’s performance reflects superficial pattern memorization rather than true object-level reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Does the Model Generalize to Varied Instructions?</h3>

<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:55%;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03827v1/language_ood_1.png" id="S5.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:262/71;" width="262" height="71" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Original task.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03827v1/language_ood_2.png" id="S5.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:262/71;" width="262" height="71" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Semantic perturbation: Grab salad dressing and set it in basket.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.03827v1/language_ood_3.png" id="S5.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:262/72;" width="262" height="72" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S5.F5.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Meaningless task: fdsgfdsgsd or <math id="S5.F5.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xxx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xxx</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pick up salad dressing and place it in basket.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated whether the VLA model robustly follows natural language instructions. As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.4 Does the Model Generalize to Varied Instructions? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ LIBERO-PRO: Towards Robust and Fair Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Models Beyond Memorization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the model successfully executed the original task (“pick up the salad dressing and place it in the basket”) and also completed the task when the instruction was reformulated with synonymous expressions (“grab the salad dressing and set it in the basket”). Although this outcome may appear to suggest robustness to linguistic variation, when the instruction was replaced with meaningless input (e.g., “xxx”), the model produced the same action sequence, again retrieving and executing the pattern of placing the salad dressing into the basket. This observation indicates that the apparent robustness does not arise from genuine semantic comprehension. Rather, the model relies predominantly on visual context and memorized action trajectories from training, while disregarding the linguistic content of the input. Consequently, the model’s consistent behavior across both paraphrased and nonsensical instructions should not be regarded as successful generalization to semantic perturbations, but instead as evidence of its inability to ground or interpret instructions, revealing an overdependence on rote pattern execution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>How Sensitive is the Model to Object Placement?</h3>

<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:55%;">
<p id="S5.F6.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F6.3.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2510.03827v1/perburtation.png" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:262/230;" width="262" height="230" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S5.F6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Success rates of OpenVLA, pi0, and pi0.5 under object position perturbations.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although all three models achieve around 98% accuracy under the standard LIBERO, our perturbation experiments reveal significant robustness gaps that remain hidden in the original evaluation. As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.5 How Sensitive is the Model to Object Placement? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ LIBERO-PRO: Towards Robust and Fair Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Models Beyond Memorization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, the models are highly sensitive to the initial position of objects: for both OpenVLA and pi0, performance collapses once displacement exceeds 0.2 units, dropping to zero success, while pi0.5—benefiting from a stronger architecture and broader training data—exhibits improved resilience and sustains non-trivial accuracy up to 0.4 units before also collapsing to zero. These results indicate that high scores do not guarantee reliable performance in practice, as even small variations such as placing a cup in a slightly different position can cause complete task failure, underscoring the necessity of LIBERO-PRO to systematically reveal such robustness gaps and provide a more faithful comparison across models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6 </span>Is the Model Truly Understanding Tasks?</h3>

<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:55%;">
<p id="S5.F7.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F7.3.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2510.03827v1/t_perturbation.png" id="S5.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:262/87;" width="262" height="87" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S5.F7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The model successfully executed two independent instructions, but failed to execute the combined instruction.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess whether the model demonstrates genuine task comprehension or merely memorizes action trajectories, we designed a sequence of three progressively structured tasks (as shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.6 Is the Model Truly Understanding Tasks? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ LIBERO-PRO: Towards Robust and Fair Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Models Beyond Memorization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). In the first task, the agent is instructed to pick up the bowl and place it on the stove, and the model executes this sequence successfully, indicating its ability to reproduce simple manipulation behaviors. In the second task, the instruction is to turn on the stove, and again the model succeeds, suggesting competence in carrying out isolated atomic actions. However, in the third task, which composes these two operations into a longer sequence—first picking up the bowl, then placing it on the stove, and finally turning the stove on—the model consistently fails. Rather than demonstrating adaptive reasoning over the composed instruction, the model replays spurious trajectories resembling those seen during training, leading to unsuccessful execution. This discrepancy reveals a critical limitation: the model does not truly understand either the semantic goal of the task or the causal relationships between subtasks, but instead exhibits rote reproduction of memorized patterns from the training distribution, and the failure on even modestly composed instructions underscores the fragility of current approaches and highlights the need for evaluation protocols that probe beyond single-step or template-like behaviors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, our study reveals that the current LIBERO evaluation protocol is fundamentally flawed: by reusing identical training and evaluation tasks with only imperceptible perturbations, it fails to measure genuine capability. Reported accuracies above 90% largely reflect rote memorization of fixed mappings from the training set, rather than true comprehension of instructions or acquisition of robust action strategies. We call on the community to move beyond misleading evaluation practices and adopt our LIBERO-PRO benchmark, which provides a principled and effective framework for assessing the real generalization and understanding capabilities of VLA models.</p>
</div>
</section>
